<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Continuation War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Continuation War was the conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> from June 1941 to September 1944, fought alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s invasion of the Soviet Union. Finland recovered the lands lost in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> and occupied much of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrozavodsk">Petrozavodsk</a>. After a Soviet offensive in 1944 it signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> Armistice, ceding Karelia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petsamo">Petsamo</a> but remaining independent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/continuation-war.jpg" alt="Continuation War" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sot.virk. Uuno Laukka · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finnish_soldiers_1944.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a> of 1939 to 1940 ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-peace-treaty">Moscow Peace Treaty</a>. Finland distrusted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> afterwards, but the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> ended any Western orientation in its foreign policy. On 31 July 1940 Hitler ordered planning for an attack on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and Germany reversed its refusal to sell arms to Finland and allowed its troops to transit through Finland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Its leaders said they would fight only to redress the balance of the 1940 treaty and described themselves as co-belligerents rather than allies of Germany, though President Risto Ryti envisioned a Greater Finland and Mannerheim often called the war an anti-Communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>. Historians still disagree on whether Finland was an ally of Nazi Germany.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>German forces invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> on 22 June 1941, and Finnish troops occupied the demilitarised Åland Islands. After a Soviet air raid on Finnish cities on 25 June, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> declared war. By September 1941 it had regained the territory conceded in 1940, and it went on into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-karelia">East Karelia</a>, halting some 30–32 km from the centre of Leningrad. Historians debate how far Finland took part in the siege of the city. In Lapland the joint German–Finnish Operation Silver Fox, begun on 29 June, failed to take Murmansk or cut the Kirov Railway.</p><p>From 19 July 1941 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> ran a military administration in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-karelia">East Karelia</a>, intending to prepare its incorporation into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>; almost 30% of the remaining Russian civilians were interned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>. On 9 June 1944 the Soviet Leningrad Front began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyborg">Vyborg</a>–Petrozavodsk Offensive. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> retook Viipuri on 20 June and Petrozavodsk by 28 June, though the Finnish army halted the offensive in August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A ceasefire took effect on 4 September 1944, and the Moscow Armistice was accepted by the Finnish Parliament on 19 September. Finland restored the borders of 1940, ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petsamo">Petsamo</a>, leased the Porkkala Peninsula to the Soviets, paid war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a>, held war-responsibility trials and banned fascist groups. A Soviet-led Allied Control Commission oversaw the terms. The demand that German troops leave led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lapland-war">Lapland War</a> between Finland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. According to Finnish historians, Finnish forces lost 63,204 dead or missing and around 158,000 wounded.</p><p>The 1947 Paris Peace Treaty described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> as an ally of Hitlerite Germany during the war. The Finnish Jewish community of about 2,000 had full civil rights, but in November 1942 officials secretly deported eight Jewish refugees to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a>, of whom only one survived; the government then refused to hand over any more.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Continuation War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Finland joined the German attack on the Soviet Union in June 1941 and fought the <b>Continuation War</b>, recapturing and advancing beyond the lost lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Finnish forces occupied much of it, including Petrozavodsk, during the <b>Continuation War</b> of 1941-1944.</blockquote><a href="/state/karelo-finnish-soviet-socialist-republic">Karelo-Finnish Soviet Socialist Republic</a></li></ul></section>
